{
  "dom_id": "step-q130314",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+4{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
